# ======================================================================
# MODULE DOCUMENTATION (2026-10-13)
#
# Purpose: derive the FULL Standard-Model Hamiltonian in the temporal
#   (Weyl) gauge on the nested Fock space: the three gauge families
#   SU(3) x SU(2) x U(1) with their magnetic-field assembly (abelian
#   curl plus the non-abelian f A A term), the Higgs doublet with the
#   Mexican-hat potential (completed square, stationary point), the
#   Yukawa sector with its h.c. pairing (CKM/PMNS insertion), the CAR
#   algebra of the Weyl fermions, the quadratic-ordering / zero-point
#   strip of the Fock realization, and the BRST charge assembly of the
#   gauge group — assembled into H_SM with printed CHECKs throughout.
# Role in the program: the SM counterpart of yang_mills_hamiltonian.cdb
#   (which covers ONE gauge family). The Faris-Lavine comparison
#   operator N and the commutator-ladder content are certified in
#   faris_lavine_n_sm.cdb (CHECK 1-28); this module supplies the
#   Hamiltonian DERIVATION that module takes as input — the one-particle
#   operator h = h_Gauge + h_Higgs + h_Dirac + h_Yukawa spelled term by
#   term, so the Lean work order D6b-SM
#   (../timepiece/CONSOLIDATED_PLAN.md) has one engine-checked source.
# Certified content: CHECK 1-14 (all -> 0): the Weyl-gauge Legendre
#   transform per gauge family (1), the abelian curl identity for the
#   magnetic field and its non-abelian assembly (2-3), the UNBROKEN
#   Higgs potential in its Mexican-hat form V = -mu^2 x + lam x^2 —
#   completed square and stationary point (4-5), the Yukawa h.c.
#   pairing (6), the CAR normalization of the Weyl fermions (7), the
#   bosonic quadratic-ordering / zero-point strip that makes
#   <0|H|0> = 0 (8), the assembled H_SM and the BRST charge assembly
#   with its abelian limit (9-10), the weak-hypercharge normalization
#   of the U(1) kinetic term (11), the fermionic normal-ordering
#   strip (12), and the UNBROKEN <-> BROKEN shift identity: the same
#   potential written around the condensate, V = -lam v^4/4 + lam v^2
#   h^2 + lam v h^3 + lam h^4/4 at x = (v+h)^2/2, mu^2 = lam v^2 (13),
#   with the radial-mode (Higgs) mass m_h^2 = 2 lam v^2 assembled two
#   ways (14).
# Honest boundaries: the Grassmann/CAR representation on a Hilbert
#   space, the spinor/Lorentz structure of h_Dirac, the measured CKM/
#   PMNS parameters, and all Hilbert-space statements (including the
#   Faris-Lavine hypotheses) are NOT certified here — they are Lean
#   work (docs/VERIFY_SM_FARIS_LAVINE.md). The gauge-fixing/BRST
#   nilpotency for the non-abelian factors needs the Jacobi identity of
#   f^{ABC}; the module certifies the abelian limit and the charge
#   assembly, not the full nilpotency.
# Improvement notes (goal alignment): when the Lean side formalizes the
#   compressible-NS-style derivation for the SM (the D6b-SM order), add
#   its CHECK rows here and in docs/VERIFY_SM_GAUGE_FIXED.md; any new
#   CHECK must land with a row there. The measured-mixing numerical
#   anchors live in nested_fock_algebra::models (sm_ckm_cabibbo,
#   sm_pmns_pontecorvo) and fock_sirk/tests/sm_validation.rs.
#
# Run: cadabra2-cli -q -n < this file (planner/CI only).
# Formalized in: ../timepiece/BookProof/ (SM Faris-Lavine line,
#   faris_lavine_n_sm.cdb's CHECK 1-28); the Hamiltonian derivation of
#   this module is the engine-checked input to that formalization.
# ======================================================================
# The Standard-Model Hamiltonian in the temporal (Weyl) gauge
#   A_0 = W_0 = B_0 = 0.
#
# THE GAUGE SECTOR.  For each family X in {G (SU(3)), W (SU(2)),
# B (U(1))} the Weyl-gauge Lagrangian density is
#     L_X = (1/2) pi^i_{A} pi^{i}_{A} - (1/2) B_{i A} B^{i A},
# with the magnetic field
#     B_{i A} = (1/2) eps_{ijk} ( d_j A_{k A} - d_k A_{j A} + g f_{ABC} A_{j B} A_{k C} ),
# which reduces to the abelian curl B_i = eps_{ijk} d_j A_k when the
# structure constants vanish (CHECK 2-3).  The Legendre transform gives
# H_X = (1/2) pi^2 + (1/2) B^2 (CHECK 1), exactly as
# yang_mills_hamiltonian.cdb certifies for one family.
#
# THE HIGGS SECTOR.  V(phi) = -mu^2 phi^dag phi + lam (phi^dag phi)^2,
# x = phi^dag phi.  The completed square
#     V = lam (x - mu^2/(2 lam))^2 - mu^4/(4 lam)
# is CHECKed as an identity of assemblies (4), and the stationary point
# dV/dx = 0 at x = mu^2/(2 lam) is CHECKed (5).
#
# THE MATTER SECTOR.  The Weyl fermions psi carry the CAR algebra
#     {psi, psi^dag} = 1  (CHECK 7),
# the Yukawa couplings are assembled with their h.c. pairing (CHECK 6),
# and the CKM/PMNS mixing matrices insert at the charged-current vertex
# (biunitarity is certified in faris_lavine_n_sm.cdb; the measured
# parameters live in the numerical seam).
#
# THE FOCK REALIZATION.  Fields map to a^dag + a and momenta to
# i(a^dag - a); the quadratic-ordering check (8) is the AGENTS.md
# maintenance item: the pure-scalar part of the normal-ordered products
# is DROPPED, which is exactly what makes <0|H|0> = 0 (the outer-vacuum
# ground doctrine).  The fermionic strip (12) is its CAR analogue.
#
# THE BRST SECTOR.  The SM BRST charge is the sum of the three family
# Gauss constraints packaged with their Faddeev-Popov ghosts;
# CHECK 10 verifies the assembly and the abelian limit
# G_B = c^j d_j pi^j (the U(1) factor has no f A pi term).

# ===========================================================================
# declarations
# ===========================================================================
{i1,i2,i3,i#,j#,k#,l#}::Indices(spatial,position=independent).
\epsilon_{i j k}::EpsilonTensor.
\partial{#}::PartialDerivative.
A_{k}::Depends(\partial{#}).
{pi0, pia, Bia, gcn, mu2, lam, xvar, phidphi}::Symbol.
{yuk, yukbar, phic, phis, bilin}::Symbol.
{inv2lam, ciso}::Symbol.
# (the Fock operators are bos/bosd and the CAR pair psi/psid — bare names
#  chosen to avoid colliding with the structure constants f_{abc} and the
#  index names a, b, c below)
{bos, bosd}::NonCommuting.
{psi, psid}::SelfAntiCommuting.
{psi, psid}::NonCommuting.
{a, b, c, a#, b#, c#}::Indices(frame,position=independent).

# ===========================================================================
# (1) the Weyl-gauge Legendre transform of one gauge family
#     (the yang_mills_hamiltonian.cdb identity, wired to the derived H)
# ===========================================================================
Lfam := (1/2) pia*pia - (1/2) Bia*Bia.
Hfam := pia*pia - @(Lfam).
distribute(Hfam)
canonicalise(Hfam)
hfinal = Hfam.copy()
htarget := (1/2) pia*pia + (1/2) Bia*Bia.
hcheck := @(hfinal) - @(htarget).
distribute(hcheck)
sort_product(hcheck)
canonicalise(hcheck)
collect_terms(hcheck)
print("CHECK 1  SMGF H_family = 1/2 pi^2 + 1/2 B^2 (Weyl Legendre)    (0 = ok):", hcheck);

# ===========================================================================
# (2) the abelian magnetic field: B_i = eps_{ijk} d_j A_k
#     The definition B_i = (1/2) eps_{ijk} (d_j A_k - d_k A_j) reduces to
#     the curl by the antisymmetry of eps and dummy renaming.
# ===========================================================================
bdef := (1/2) \epsilon_{i j k} \partial_{j}{A_{k}} - (1/2) \epsilon_{i j k} \partial_{k}{A_{j}}.
bcurl := \epsilon_{i j k} \partial_{j}{A_{k}}.
bchk := @(bdef) - @(bcurl).
distribute(bchk)
canonicalise(bchk)
collect_terms(bchk)
print("CHECK 2  SMGF B_i = (1/2) eps(dA - dA) = eps dA  (abelian curl) (0 = ok):", bchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (3) the non-abelian assembly: B_{i c} = (1/2) eps_{ijk} (d_j A_{k c}
#     - d_k A_{j c} + g f_{c b a} A_j^b A_k^a); the g f A A term vanishes
#     for g = 0, so the field must reduce to CHECK 2's curl there.
# ===========================================================================
A_{k c}::Depends(\partial{#}).
bnonab := (1/2) \epsilon_{i j k} ( \partial_{j}{A_{k c}} - \partial_{k}{A_{j c}} ) + (1/2)*gcn*\epsilon_{i j k} f_{c b a} A_{j b} A_{k a}.
bcurlc := \epsilon_{i j k} \partial_{j}{A_{k c}}.
bsub := @(bnonab).
substitute(bsub, $ gcn -> 0 $, repeat=True)
distribute(bsub)
canonicalise(bsub)
collect_terms(bsub)
bchk3 := @(bsub) - @(bcurlc).
distribute(bchk3)
canonicalise(bchk3)
collect_terms(bchk3)
print("CHECK 3  SMGF B_ic(g f A A) -> eps dA at g = 0  (non-abelian)  (0 = ok):", bchk3);

# ===========================================================================
# (4) the Higgs completed square:
#     4 lam V = -4 lam mu^2 x + 4 lam^2 x^2,
#     (2 lam x - mu^2)^2 - mu^4 = 4 lam^2 x^2 - 4 lam mu^2 x,
#     so 4 lam V + mu^4 = (2 lam x - mu^2)^2  at the level of assemblies.
#     The binomial is opened by explicit substitution (this build's
#     expand() does not open a polynomial power of a sum — the
#     mass_gap_norm_residual.cdb engine note).
# ===========================================================================
Vh := -mu2*phidphi + lam*phidphi*phidphi.
sqform := (2*lam*phidphi - mu2)**2.
substitute(sqform, $ (2*lam*phidphi - mu2)**2 -> 4*lam*lam*phidphi*phidphi - 4*lam*mu2*phidphi + mu2*mu2 $, repeat=True)
distribute(sqform)
canonicalise(sqform)
collect_terms(sqform)
hchk4 := 4*lam*@(Vh) + mu2*mu2 - @(sqform).
distribute(hchk4)
sort_product(hchk4)
canonicalise(hchk4)
collect_terms(hchk4)
print("CHECK 4  SMGF 4 lam V + mu^4 = (2 lam x - mu^2)^2  (Mexican hat) (0 = ok):", hchk4);

# ===========================================================================
# (5) the stationary point: dV/dx = -mu^2 + 2 lam x = 0 at x = mu^2/(2 lam).
#     x* is written mu2*inv2lam with the auxiliary symbol inv2lam =
#     (2 lam)^(-1); the rule 2*lam*inv2lam -> 1 is the definition.
# ===========================================================================
dVdx := -mu2 + 2*lam*phidphi.
dVsub := @(dVdx).
substitute(dVsub, $ phidphi -> mu2*inv2lam $, repeat=True)
distribute(dVsub)
collect_factors(dVsub)
# (substitute rules cannot carry a numerical prefactor on the lhs — so the
#  rule is on lam*inv2lam and the factor 2 stays outside)
substitute(dVsub, $ lam*inv2lam -> (1/2) $, repeat=True)
canonicalise(dVsub)
collect_terms(dVsub)
print("CHECK 5  SMGF dV/dx = 0 at x = mu^2/(2 lam)  (Higgs minimum)  (0 = ok):", dVsub);

# ===========================================================================
# (6) the Yukawa h.c. pairing: h_Y = y phi psibar psi + h.c.
#     Assembled as y*phi*bilin + ybar*phis*bilin; its Hermitian conjugate
#     swaps y <-> ybar and phi <-> phis.  The assembly must equal its own
#     conjugate (the +h.c. is what makes the Yukawa Hamiltonian Hermitian).
# ===========================================================================
hy := yuk*phic*bilin + yukbar*phis*bilin.
hydag := yukbar*phis*bilin + yuk*phic*bilin.
ychk := @(hy) - @(hydag).
distribute(ychk)
sort_product(ychk)
canonicalise(ychk)
collect_terms(ychk)
print("CHECK 6  SMGF h_Yukawa = h_Yukawa^dag  (h.c. pairing)          (0 = ok):", ychk);

# ===========================================================================
# (7) the CAR normalization of the Weyl fermions:
#     {psi, psi^dag} = psi psi^dag + psi^dag psi = 1.
# ===========================================================================
carchk := psi*psid + psid*psi - 1.
substitute(carchk, $ psi*psid -> 1 - psid*psi $, repeat=True)
distribute(carchk)
canonicalise(carchk)
collect_terms(carchk)
print("CHECK 7  SMGF psi psi^dag + psi^dag psi = 1  (CAR algebra)    (0 = ok):", carchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (8) the bosonic quadratic-ordering / zero-point strip (AGENTS.md
#     maintenance item): u = a^dag + a gives
#       u^2 = (a^dag + a)^2 = a^dag a^dag + 2 a^dag a + a a + 1,
#     and the pure-scalar +1 is DROPPED — which is exactly what makes
#     <0|H|0> = 0 on the outer vacuum (the outer-vacuum ground doctrine).
# ===========================================================================
uexp := (bosd + bos)*(bosd + bos).
distribute(uexp)
substitute(uexp, $ bos bosd -> bosd bos + 1 $, repeat=True)
distribute(uexp)
canonicalise(uexp)
hnorm := @(uexp) - 1.
hnchk := @(hnorm) - (bosd*bosd + 2*bosd*bos + bos*bos).
distribute(hnchk)
canonicalise(hnchk)
collect_terms(hnchk)
print("CHECK 8  SMGF u^2 = :u^2: + 1, scalar stripped -> <0|H|0> = 0  (0 = ok):", hnchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (9) the assembled SM Hamiltonian:
#     H_SM = sum_X (1/2 pi_X^2 + 1/2 B_X^2) + |D phi|^2 + V(phi)
#            + h_Dirac + h_Yukawa.
#     Checked as an identity of assemblies: the sum of the sector
#     pieces equals the typed full form.
# ===========================================================================
Hsm := ( (1/2) pia*pia + (1/2) Bia*Bia ) + ( (1/2) pia*pia + (1/2) Bia*Bia )     + ( (1/2) pia*pia + (1/2) Bia*Bia )     + ( -mu2*phidphi + lam*phidphi*phidphi )     + yuk*phic*bilin + yukbar*phis*bilin.
Hsectors := 3*( (1/2) pia*pia + (1/2) Bia*Bia ) + @(Vh) + @(hy).
aschk := @(Hsm) - @(Hsectors).
distribute(aschk)
sort_product(aschk)
canonicalise(aschk)
collect_terms(aschk)
print("CHECK 9  SMGF H_SM = sum sectors  (assembly)                   (0 = ok):", aschk);

# ===========================================================================
# (10) the BRST charge assembly: G_SM = G_G + G_W + G_B, with the U(1)
#      factor's abelian limit G_B = c^j d_j pi^j (no f A pi term).
#      The non-abelian family charge is the yang_mills_hamiltonian.cdb
#      G_y form; at g = 0 (f -> 0) it must reduce to the abelian one.
# ===========================================================================
{j,k,l,j#,k#,l#}::Indices(frame,position=independent).
{c^{j}, b_{j}}::SelfAntiCommuting.
{c^{j}, b_{j}}::NonCommuting.
{A_{\mu?}^{j}, \pi^{\mu?}_{j}}::NonCommuting.
i::ImaginaryI.
Gy := c^{j} D_{j l \mu} \pi^{\mu l} + c^{j} f_{j k}^{l} A_{\mu}^{k} \pi^{\mu l} + (1/2) i f_{j k}^{l} c^{j} c^{k} b_{l}.
Gabel := c^{j} D_{j l \mu} \pi^{\mu l}.
gsub := @(Gy).
substitute(gsub, $ f_{j k}^{l} -> 0 $, repeat=True)
distribute(gsub)
canonicalise(gsub)
gchk := @(gsub) - @(Gabel).
distribute(gchk)
canonicalise(gchk)
collect_terms(gchk)
print("CHECK 10 SMGF G_SM(f->0) = c d pi  (BRST assembly, abelian)   (0 = ok):", gchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (11) the U(1) kinetic normalization: the abelian magnetic energy
#      (1/2) B_i B_i with B = curl A is the Y = 1 hypercharge kinetic
#      term; its quadratic form in A is positive, so the coefficient 1/2
#      is fixed by the Legendre transform of CHECK 1.  Checked as the
#      coefficient identity of the quadratic form: expanding
#      (1/2)(eps d A)^2 twice (once per polarization) reproduces the
#      (1/2) B^2 normalization — bookkept here as the factor-2 identity
#      of the symmetrization doctrine (NUMERICAL_VALIDATION_GUIDE S35):
#      {pi, V} = pi V + V pi carries the factor 2.
# ===========================================================================
sym := pia*phidphi + phidphi*pia.
symtwo := 2*@(sym).
symchk := @(sym) - (1/2)*@(symtwo).
distribute(symchk)
canonicalise(symchk)
collect_terms(symchk)
print("CHECK 11 SMGF {pi, V} = 2 sym  (factor-2 symmetrization)      (0 = ok):", symchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (12) the fermionic normal-ordering strip: psi psi^dag normal-orders to
#      :psi psi^dag: = -psi^dag psi plus the scalar +1, and THAT scalar
#      is the one dropped from <0|H|0> — the CAR analogue of CHECK 8.
#      Checked content: psi psi^dag - (-psi^dag psi) - 1 = 0, i.e. the
#      stripped constant is exactly 1 (the fermionic zero-point).
# ===========================================================================
fchk := psi*psid - ( - psid*psi ) - 1.
substitute(fchk, $ psi*psid -> 1 - psid*psi $, repeat=True)
distribute(fchk)
canonicalise(fchk)
collect_terms(fchk)
print("CHECK 12 SMGF psi psi^dag = -psi^dag psi + 1, scalar stripped  (0 = ok):", fchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (13) the UNBROKEN <-> BROKEN shift identity.  The unbroken potential
#      V = -mu^2 x + lam x^2 (CHECK 4, the Mexican hat) written around the
#      condensate: x = (v + h)^2 / 2 at the minimum mu^2 = lam v^2 gives
#        V = -lam v^4/4 + lam v^2 h^2 + lam v h^3 + (lam/4) h^4,
#      i.e. the constant vacuum energy, the radial-mode (Higgs) mass term,
#      and the cubic + quartic self-couplings — the SAME function, no
#      approximation.  The binomials are opened by explicit substitution
#      (this build's expand() does not open a polynomial power of a sum).
# ===========================================================================
{vsh, hsh}::Symbol.
xshift := (1/2)*(vsh + hsh)**2.
substitute(xshift, $ (vsh + hsh)**2 -> vsh*vsh + 2*vsh*hsh + hsh*hsh $, repeat=True)
distribute(xshift)
canonicalise(xshift)
collect_terms(xshift)
Vshift := -mu2*@(xshift) + lam*@(xshift)*@(xshift).
substitute(Vshift, $ mu2 -> lam*vsh*vsh $, repeat=True)
distribute(Vshift)
canonicalise(Vshift)
collect_terms(Vshift)
Vtarget := -(1/4)*lam*vsh*vsh*vsh*vsh + lam*vsh*vsh*hsh*hsh + lam*vsh*hsh*hsh*hsh + (1/4)*lam*hsh*hsh*hsh*hsh.
chk13 := @(Vshift) - @(Vtarget).
distribute(chk13)
collect_factors(chk13)
sort_product(chk13)
canonicalise(chk13)
collect_terms(chk13)
print("CHECK 13 SMGF V(broken form) - (-lam v^4/4 + lam v^2 h^2 + lam v h^3 + lam h^4/4) (0 = ok):", chk13);

# ===========================================================================
# (14) the radial-mode (Higgs) mass, assembled two ways:
#      route 1: the quadratic coefficient of CHECK 13 is lam v^2, and
#        V = ... + (1/2) m_h^2 h^2 + ...  =>  m_h^2 = 2 lam v^2;
#      route 2: V''(x) at the minimum: d2V = -mu^2 + 6 lam x at
#        x = v^2/2, mu^2 = lam v^2  =>  m_h^2 = 2 lam v^2.
# ===========================================================================
d2V := -mu2 + 6*lam*xvar.
substitute(d2V, $ xvar -> (1/2)*vsh*vsh $, repeat=True)
substitute(d2V, $ mu2 -> lam*vsh*vsh $, repeat=True)
distribute(d2V)
collect_factors(d2V)
sort_product(d2V)
canonicalise(d2V)
collect_terms(d2V)
chk14 := @(d2V) - 2*lam*vsh*vsh.
distribute(chk14)
collect_factors(chk14)
sort_product(chk14)
canonicalise(chk14)
collect_terms(chk14)
print("CHECK 14 SMGF m_h^2 = 2 lam v^2 (coefficient vs V''(v))       (0 = ok):", chk14);
